docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2708:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:594:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01S8Y5XKPCwKKCSpELb6osGQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KdjKT5Ayp8h268eo8xjg8C","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks.json","offset":1275,"limit":380},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":490,"cache_read_input_tokens":49046,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":490,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":46,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"0691c32b-04e7-4675-9277-612d10b11495","request_id":"req_011Cb7MoEQ2k1jWZQx1STJuq","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2709:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:597:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012dWuByvFthfkPZ5xS5UtsP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013h33HXF8JPQmJW1Fsfdbgi","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md","content":"# T27 — Phase 4 — Task intent classifier\n\n**Phase:** 4\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R26\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Ranking Complexity](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#ranking-complexity)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the first stage of the Phase 4 retrieval pipeline: a deterministic, feature-based classifier that reads a user task statement and assigns it one or more intent labels (debug, refactor, explain, add-feature, test, docs, migration, performance, review, etc.). The classifier is the input to every later retrieval stage — anchor extraction biases toward intent-relevant token shapes, candidate retrieval weights source mix by intent, and the scorer applies a `task-type compatibility` signal that requires this label to exist. Per spec §Ranking Complexity, the classifier must stay feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy is layered on top, so that ranking remains explainable for the lifetime of Phase 4.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Pipeline: 1. Parse user task and classify intent.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Ranking signals: task-type compatibility, graph proximity to anchors, exact identifier match, semantic similarity, verification status, freshness, scope, evidence strength, contradiction/supersession state, past usefulness, recent successful reuse, user preference compatibility, token cost.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: task intent classifier; anchor extractor and resolver; hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events; scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness; compact response shaper; diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons; benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks.\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n> \"Risk: ranking becomes hard to reason about. Control: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\" (spec §Ranking Complexity)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R26 (Phase 3 memory-graph review verdict) — ensures retrieval can rely on typed memory streams, link tables, and verification status fields existing as designed.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` — current workflow tools that today perform implicit intent inference inline; T27 lifts that into a dedicated module.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/query/engine.rs` — current heuristic intent detection in the query path; behaviour to preserve and extend.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/lib.rs` — module registry that must expose the new `retrieval_v1` namespace.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, single source of truth — no parallel intent inference helpers); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references in code comments where behaviour is doc-anchored).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md` §Retrieval Engine pipeline step 1, §Phase 4 deliverables, and §Ranking Complexity in full so the inspectability constraint is internalized verbatim.\n2. Enumerate the intent labels the classifier must emit, drawn from spec language and current workflow tool coverage: `debug`, `refactor`, `explain`, `add_feature`, `modify_feature`, `add_test`, `update_docs`, `migration`, `performance`, `review`, `unknown`. Document each label with a short doc comment naming the workflow tools that depend on it.\n3. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/mod.rs` (new directory) that re-exports the classifier API and registers the module with `lib.rs`.\n4. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs`: a pure-function classifier `classify_intent(task_text: &str) -> IntentClassification` that returns a primary label, optional secondary labels, per-feature scores, and the literal features that fired (lemma matches, imperative-verb hits, error-keyword hits, file/symbol token shapes, question-form hits). Features must be a finite, documented list — no learned weights, no opaque model — so the result is fully inspectable per §Ranking Complexity.\n5. Surface a `diagnostics()` view on `IntentClassification` that returns `(label, contributing_features, score)` tuples for use by the diagnostic mode of T30 and the working-memory inspector of T36; this is the contract that lets the ranker explain why a label was chosen.\n6. Migrate the existing heuristic intent inference in `query/engine.rs` to call the new classifier instead of duplicating logic; remove the old inline heuristic in the same commit per \"single source of truth\" in `coding.md`.\n7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs` covering: every label appears for at least one canonical task statement; ambiguous tasks return multiple labels with documented tie-break; empty/whitespace input returns `unknown` without panic; long input is truncated at a documented boundary without losing the leading verb; diagnostics output round-trips through serde.\n8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings in the new module before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` — feature-based intent classifier with documented label set and diagnostics view.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for label coverage, ambiguity handling, edge inputs, diagnostics serialization.\n\n(Implicitly required to make the module compile: `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/mod.rs` with module declarations, registered from `lib.rs`.)\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` — confirms the classifier file exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::intent_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every documented label and edge case is covered.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (`wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. The classifier must use early returns and table-driven feature evaluation; no nested match pyramids.\n- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: the legacy heuristic intent inference in `query/engine.rs` is removed in this commit; no parallel classifier remains.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent.\n- Doc citation: where the classifier comments explain a behaviour mandated by `## Ranking Complexity` in the plan doc, the comment cites that exact heading per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` \"Markdown Heading References\".\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 4 deliverable \"task intent classifier\" punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] Legacy inline intent heuristic in `query/engine.rs` migrated to the new module in the same commit (single source of truth).\n- [ ] Diagnostics view is exported and exercised by a test, ready to be consumed by T30 (scoring) and T36 (working-memory inspector).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":187,"cache_read_input_tokens":45959,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":187,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"7b3a9aaf-37d5-4241-8bbe-3572b8a62729","request_id":"req_011Cb7MkGmZvqNoB2E9TvBXh","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2711:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:631:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Pnh5gggscokdtYCqGBG6x","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# T02 — Phase 0 — Successor architecture overview + compatibility policy\n2\t\n3\t**Phase:** 0\n4\t**Type:** docs\n5\t**Model class:** balanced\n6\t**Depends on:** T01\n7\t**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 0: Fork Foundation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation)\n8\t**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n9\t\n10\t## Goal\n11\t\n12\tThis task produces the two foundational design documents that govern every later phase: the successor architecture overview (workspace graph, event log, memory graph as three first-class sources of truth) and the MCP compatibility policy (which existing tools remain stable, which gain additive fields, which are deprecated with shims). It matters because spec §Design Thesis requires all three substrates to share identity, storage, and ranking primitives — without a single overview doc, each phase risks diverging. The compatibility policy is binding for every Phase 8 tool redesign and protects existing assistant clients while the substrate evolves.\n13\t\n14\t## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n15\t\n16\t> \"The correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth: 1. Workspace graph: files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries. 2. Event log: every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome. 3. Memory graph: durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\"\n17\t\n18\t> \"Phase 0 Deliverables: ... architecture docs copied and revised ... compatibility policy for existing MCP tools.\"\n19\t\n20\t## Inputs\n21\t\n22\t- Dependency outputs: `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md` from T01 (decides the branch/repo target for these docs).\n23\t- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n24\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n25\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n26\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n27\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n28\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n29\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` (current 43 registered tools)\n30\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` (current workflow tools)\n31\t- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (no broken windows, single source of truth, citation discipline); `AGENTS.md` \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\".\n32\t\n33\t## Steps\n34\t\n35\t1. Read T01's decision doc and the spec sections §Design Thesis, §System Architecture, §MCP Surface, §MCP Tool Contract Principles.\n36\t2. In `2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md`, write a `## Workspace graph` section enumerating the node and edge families from spec §Workspace Graph and mapping each to the existing or planned crate.\n37\t3. Write a `## Event log` section summarizing the 20 event types, the append-only invariant, compaction snapshots, and the hot-path budget (≤5ms P99) from spec §Event Log and §Phase 2 DoD.\n38\t4. Write a `## Memory graph` section covering the memory classes, link types, evidence model, and the `CounterMemory` distinction from spec §Memory Graph.\n39\t5. Add a `## Shared substrate primitives` section explaining how identity, storage, and ranking are shared across the three substrates.\n40\t6. Add a `## Reference to existing docs` section linking forward to the prior `2026-04-11-*` architecture docs without duplicating their content.\n41\t7. In `2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`, enumerate every existing MCP tool by name and assign each a status: `stable` (no breaking change), `additive` (gains optional fields only), `redesigned-with-shim` (legacy name keeps responding with a compatibility shim), or `deprecated-with-deadline`.\n42\t8. Write a `## Backward compatibility` section stating the binding rule: every existing assistant client must continue to function for a full phase cycle after any tool's redesign lands. Include the shim removal protocol.\n43\t9. Cross-link the two docs and reference the spec heading anchors precisely.\n44\t\n45\t## Files expected\n46\t\n47\t- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — high-level architecture overview anchoring the three substrates and shared primitives for every later phase.\n48\t- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — per-tool stability classification and the backward-compatibility rule that binds Phase 8 redesign.\n49\t\n50\t## Verification\n51\t\n52\t- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the architecture doc exists.\n53\t- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — confirms the compatibility policy exists.\n54\t- `grep -q '## Workspace graph' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the workspace graph substrate is documented under its own heading.\n55\t- `grep -q '## Event log' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the event log substrate is documented under its own heading.\n56\t- `grep -q '## Memory graph' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the memory graph substrate is documented under its own heading.\n57\t- `grep -q '## Backward compatibility' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — confirms the backward-compatibility rule is stated under a dedicated heading rather than implied.\n58\t\n59\t## Coding-standard checks\n60\t\n61\t- File length ≤800 lines per file (verify with `wc -l docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`).\n62\t- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — not applicable (docs task).\n63\t- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n64\t- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n65\t- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming — applies to any pseudo-signatures shown in the docs.\n66\t- For schema changes: not applicable (no schema changes in this task).\n67\t- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Design Thesis`, `## System Architecture`, and `## MCP Surface` headings from the spec; reference `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-*.md` headings rather than duplicating content (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n68\t\n69\t## Definition of done\n70\t\n71\t- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n72\t- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n73\t- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n74\t- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 0 punted to a later phase.\n75\t- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"8176b57f-8fee-494d-b55e-cffaa1f6f7f0","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:34:18.997Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2713:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:637:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CboDm5JFPZDHUNAneCuUyG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# T27 — Phase 4 — Task intent classifier\n2\t\n3\t**Phase:** 4\n4\t**Type:** backend\n5\t**Model class:** balanced\n6\t**Depends on:** R26\n7\t**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Ranking Complexity](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#ranking-complexity)\n8\t**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n9\t\n10\t## Goal\n11\t\n12\tThis task delivers the first stage of the Phase 4 retrieval pipeline: a deterministic, feature-based classifier that reads a user task statement and assigns it one or more intent labels (debug, refactor, explain, add-feature, test, docs, migration, performance, review, etc.). The classifier is the input to every later retrieval stage — anchor extraction biases toward intent-relevant token shapes, candidate retrieval weights source mix by intent, and the scorer applies a `task-type compatibility` signal that requires this label to exist. Per spec §Ranking Complexity, the classifier must stay feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy is layered on top, so that ranking remains explainable for the lifetime of Phase 4.\n13\t\n14\t## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n15\t\n16\t> \"Pipeline: 1. Parse user task and classify intent.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n17\t\n18\t> \"Ranking signals: task-type compatibility, graph proximity to anchors, exact identifier match, semantic similarity, verification status, freshness, scope, evidence strength, contradiction/supersession state, past usefulness, recent successful reuse, user preference compatibility, token cost.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n19\t\n20\t> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: task intent classifier; anchor extractor and resolver; hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events; scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness; compact response shaper; diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons; benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks.\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n21\t\n22\t> \"Risk: ranking becomes hard to reason about. Control: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\" (spec §Ranking Complexity)\n23\t\n24\t## Inputs\n25\t\n26\t- Dependency outputs: R26 (Phase 3 memory-graph review verdict) — ensures retrieval can rely on typed memory streams, link tables, and verification status fields existing as designed.\n27\t- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n28\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` — current workflow tools that today perform implicit intent inference inline; T27 lifts that into a dedicated module.\n29\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/query/engine.rs` — current heuristic intent detection in the query path; behaviour to preserve and extend.\n30\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/lib.rs` — module registry that must expose the new `retrieval_v1` namespace.\n31\t- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, single source of truth — no parallel intent inference helpers); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references in code comments where behaviour is doc-anchored).\n32\t\n33\t## Steps\n34\t\n35\t1. Read `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md` §Retrieval Engine pipeline step 1, §Phase 4 deliverables, and §Ranking Complexity in full so the inspectability constraint is internalized verbatim.\n36\t2. Enumerate the intent labels the classifier must emit, drawn from spec language and current workflow tool coverage: `debug`, `refactor`, `explain`, `add_feature`, `modify_feature`, `add_test`, `update_docs`, `migration`, `performance`, `review`, `unknown`. Document each label with a short doc comment naming the workflow tools that depend on it.\n37\t3. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/mod.rs` (new directory) that re-exports the classifier API and registers the module with `lib.rs`.\n38\t4. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs`: a pure-function classifier `classify_intent(task_text: &str) -> IntentClassification` that returns a primary label, optional secondary labels, per-feature scores, and the literal features that fired (lemma matches, imperative-verb hits, error-keyword hits, file/symbol token shapes, question-form hits). Features must be a finite, documented list — no learned weights, no opaque model — so the result is fully inspectable per §Ranking Complexity.\n39\t5. Surface a `diagnostics()` view on `IntentClassification` that returns `(label, contributing_features, score)` tuples for use by the diagnostic mode of T30 and the working-memory inspector of T36; this is the contract that lets the ranker explain why a label was chosen.\n40\t6. Migrate the existing heuristic intent inference in `query/engine.rs` to call the new classifier instead of duplicating logic; remove the old inline heuristic in the same commit per \"single source of truth\" in `coding.md`.\n41\t7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs` covering: every label appears for at least one canonical task statement; ambiguous tasks return multiple labels with documented tie-break; empty/whitespace input returns `unknown` without panic; long input is truncated at a documented boundary without losing the leading verb; diagnostics output round-trips through serde.\n42\t8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings in the new module before stopping.\n43\t\n44\t## Files expected\n45\t\n46\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` — feature-based intent classifier with documented label set and diagnostics view.\n47\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for label coverage, ambiguity handling, edge inputs, diagnostics serialization.\n48\t\n49\t(Implicitly required to make the module compile: `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/mod.rs` with module declarations, registered from `lib.rs`.)\n50\t\n51\t## Verification\n52\t\n53\t- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` — confirms the classifier file exists at the expected path.\n54\t- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::intent_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every documented label and edge case is covered.\n55\t\n56\t## Coding-standard checks\n57\t\n58\t- File length ≤800 lines per file (`wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs`).\n59\t- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. The classifier must use early returns and table-driven feature evaluation; no nested match pyramids.\n60\t- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task).\n61\t- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n62\t- Single source of truth: the legacy heuristic intent inference in `query/engine.rs` is removed in this commit; no parallel classifier remains.\n63\t- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent.\n64\t- Doc citation: where the classifier comments explain a behaviour mandated by `## Ranking Complexity` in the plan doc, the comment cites that exact heading per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` \"Markdown Heading References\".\n65\t\n66\t## Definition of done\n67\t\n68\t- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n69\t- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n70\t- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n71\t- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 4 deliverable \"task intent classifier\" punted to a later phase.\n72\t- [ ] Legacy inline intent heuristic in `query/engine.rs` migrated to the new module in the same commit (single source of truth).\n73\t- [ ] Diagnostics view is exported and exercised by a test, ready to be consumed by T30 (scoring) and T36 (working-memory inspector).\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"8cf2a2a5-7754-4511-b4d6-77463a6d8045","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:34:22.899Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2714:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:639:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01M8uBUCTUJMj3USgaU3iXWd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CF386hStJ13LMHcfm7LVJ2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md","content":"# T39 — Phase 6 — Consolidation job runtime + proposal model (no silent writes)\n\n**Phase:** 6\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R38\n**Spec anchor:** [§Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#6-consolidation-engine), [§Phase 6: Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-6-consolidation-engine), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task stands up the consolidation engine's runtime kernel and the proposal model on which every later Phase 6 job depends. It introduces the `consolidation_jobs` table from spec §Storage Design, the bounded job queue, and a strict invariant that no consolidation job — deterministic or LLM-driven — writes durable memory directly. Every job emits a `ConsolidationProposal` record that requires explicit `apply` or `reject` before any memory state changes. Without this kernel, the LLM-driven jobs in T42 would be free to silently mutate memory, violating spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties and the §Opaque Memory Mutation risk control.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"No consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\"\n\n> \"each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\"\n\n> \"consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\"\n\n> \"Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\" (§Non-Negotiable Product Properties)\n\n> Core tables include `consolidation_jobs` (§Storage Design).\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R38 Phase 5 working-memory review — the consolidation runtime hangs off the same `.lattice/` storage layout and reuses the event-log and memory-graph identity types finalized through Phases 1–5.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — current memory store API; proposals must wrap writes through the existing store rather than bypass it.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — SQLite connection setup, WAL mode, migration conventions.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — `EventEnvelope` (`MemoryConsolidated`, consolidation failure events) emitted by job lifecycle hooks.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/` — `MemoryId`, `EventId`, `WorkspaceId` used inside proposal payloads.\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md` §1 (proposal-only invariant, bounded queue) and §6 (verification discipline).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Error handling, §Schema / model parity, §No broken windows; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references (cite §Consolidation Engine and §Storage Design rather than re-explaining behavior in code comments).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Consolidation Engine, §Phase 6, and §Storage Design in full; record the proposal-only invariant verbatim in the top-of-module doc comment on `consolidation/mod.rs`.\n2. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/mod.rs` re-exporting `proposal`, `queue`, and the `ConsolidationJobRuntime` entry point. The module doc comment must cite the exact `## 6. Consolidation Engine` and `## Storage Design` headings.\n3. Author `consolidation/schema.sql` defining the `consolidation_jobs` table (columns: `job_id PK`, `workspace_id`, `kind`, `mode` ∈ {`synchronous_post_task`, `background`, `manual_review`, `replay`}, `status` ∈ {`queued`, `running`, `proposed`, `applied`, `rejected`, `failed`, `dropped`}, `enqueued_at`, `started_at`, `finished_at`, `proposal_id NULLABLE`, `error_kind NULLABLE`, indexes on `(workspace_id, status)` and `(kind, status)`) plus a `consolidation_proposals` table (columns: `proposal_id PK`, `job_id FK`, `target_memory_id NULLABLE`, `proposal_kind`, `prior_state JSON`, `proposed_state JSON`, `evidence JSON`, `decided_at NULLABLE`, `decision` ∈ {`pending`, `applied`, `rejected`}, `decided_by NULLABLE`).\n4. In `consolidation/proposal.rs`, define `pub struct ConsolidationProposal { proposal_id, job_id, target: ProposalTarget, prior_state, proposed_state, evidence }`, the `ProposalKind` enum (e.g. `CreateMemory`, `UpdateMemory`, `MarkStale`, `Supersede`, `Demote`, `Refresh`), and the `ProposalDecision` enum. Implement `apply()` and `reject()` methods that mutate `consolidation_proposals.decision`, emit a `MemoryConsolidated` event with prior + proposed state preserved, and only then call the memory store API to materialize the change. `apply` MUST be a no-op if `decision != pending`.\n5. In `consolidation/queue.rs`, implement `BoundedJobQueue` with a configurable `max_depth` (default from `LatticeConfig`, no hard-coded literals). When `enqueue` is called and the queue is full, mark the job as `dropped` in `consolidation_jobs`, emit a `tracing::warn!` log including `workspace_id`, `kind`, and current depth, and return an `EnqueueOutcome::Dropped { reason }` — never block. Expose `depth()`, `pop()`, and `drain_for_shutdown()` for observability.\n6. Implement `ConsolidationJobRuntime` with `submit(job_spec)`, `run_due()` (polled by the daemon's scheduler), `decide(proposal_id, decision)` and `replay_from(event_id)`. The runtime never writes memory; it only writes proposals and dispatches their decisions.\n7. Wire `mod consolidation;` into `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/lib.rs` (alphabetical order) and register `consolidation/schema.sql` in the storage migration runner so the tables are created on daemon start.\n8. In `consolidation/proposal_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) a deterministic job emits a proposal but does not mutate memory, (b) `apply` materializes the change and writes a `MemoryConsolidated` event with prior + proposed state, (c) `reject` leaves memory unchanged and records the decision, (d) double-apply is a no-op, (e) the queue drops jobs once depth exceeds the bound and emits the warning log (capture via a tracing subscriber fixture), (f) the `consolidation_jobs` schema round-trips under the migration runner against a temp DB.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/mod.rs` — module root; re-exports `ConsolidationJobRuntime`, `ConsolidationProposal`, `ProposalKind`, `ProposalDecision`, `BoundedJobQueue`, `EnqueueOutcome`; doc comment cites spec §Consolidation Engine and §Storage Design.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/proposal.rs` — `ConsolidationProposal` struct, `ProposalKind`/`ProposalDecision` enums, `apply`/`reject` methods, prior-state preservation, event emission.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/queue.rs` — `BoundedJobQueue` with configurable depth, drop-with-warning behavior, observability hooks.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/schema.sql` — `consolidation_jobs` and `consolidation_proposals` tables with the exact columns and indexes the runtime requires.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/proposal_tests.rs` — unit tests for proposal lifecycle, no-silent-writes, bounded-queue drops, schema migration round-trip.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/mod.rs` — confirms the module root exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/proposal.rs` — confirms the proposal module exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib consolidation::proposal_tests` — every proposal-runtime unit test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. Split `proposal.rs` or `queue.rs` if any approaches the ceiling (e.g. extract a `decision.rs` sub-module).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. `BoundedJobQueue::enqueue` and `ConsolidationProposal::apply` are the hot spots — keep them flat with early returns.\n- Single source of truth: proposals MUST route writes through the existing memory store API (`crates/lattice-core/src/memory/`) — do not introduce a parallel write path.\n- Error handling: queue-full and apply-on-non-pending are normal control flow (typed `EnqueueOutcome` / `ApplyOutcome`), not panics. No silent `.ok()` drops on storage results.\n- Naming: `ConsolidationProposal`, `ProposalDecision`, `BoundedJobQueue` — intent over mechanism; no generic `Manager`/`Helper` suffixes.\n- Schema parity: every column on `consolidation_jobs` and `consolidation_proposals` has a typed field on the Rust struct; nullable columns map to `Option<…>`.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Observability: every proposal lifecycle transition emits a `tracing` span carrying `workspace_id`, `job_id`, `proposal_id`, and outcome. Queue drops log at `warn` per spec.\n- Doc citation: top-of-module doc comments cite `## 6. Consolidation Engine` and `## Storage Design` headings; per-method comments reference these headings only where behavior is non-obvious (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: deterministic jobs (T41), LLM jobs (T42), provenance (T43), manual review (T44), replay (T45), and integration tests (T46) are sibling tasks — but the proposal-only invariant, bounded queue, and `consolidation_jobs` schema all ship in this task.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3709,"cache_read_input_tokens":53202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3709,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015qAoQeXM7HFtFDzBbPkUNq","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"9c71f470-98b9-4480-8f0d-0c8ccdebc756","request_id":"req_011Cb7MmqeXLr2UsR7g5bqCY","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 6+7+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2715:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:642:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01XTcDiMrgdjkzP2h53pbga2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SUPWwtG9FxhbV4ptXmemxT","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T56.md","content":"# T56 — Phase 8 — Redesigned prepare_change + plan_edit + trace_scenario + diagnose_failure\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-8-workflow-engine-v2)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task redesigns the four edit-planning workflow tools (`prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `trace_scenario`, `diagnose_failure`) on top of the Phase 1–7 substrates: unified identity, event log, memory graph, retrieval pipeline, working memory, consolidation proposals, and verification status. The redesigned tools must compose those substrates in one call so that an assistant gets a complete edit-planning bundle (code anchors, docs, tests, memory with inclusion reasons, risks, verification commands) without first issuing dozens of low-level discovery calls. This is the central deliverable of Phase 8: spec §Phase 8 DoD requires \"common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\" and \"workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping.\" Without this redesign, the new substrate work in Phases 1–7 stays invisible to callers.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 8 Deliverables: redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation; workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates; one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands; workflow outcome recording integrated by default.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls; workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping.\"\n\n> \"Every assistant-facing tool should return: overview, ranked pivots, relevant context, memory highlights, event episodes where relevant, suggested next expansion, stable handles, risks or uncertainty, compact/full render choice, structured payload.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — verification statuses (`verified` | `unverified` | `stale` | `contradicted` | `superseded` | `expired` | `invalidated`) must be surfaced inside the bundle so stale memory cannot appear as trusted guidance per spec §Phase 7 DoD.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/prepare_change.rs` — current `prepare_change` heuristics, retained as a compatibility shim per the T02 compatibility policy.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/plan_edit.rs` — current patch-oriented planning.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/trace_scenario.rs` — current scenario tracer.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/diagnose_failure.rs` — current failure-diagnosis heuristics.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — current MCP tool registration; new `workflow_v2` tools register alongside legacy names.\n  - Phase 1 identity types (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `EventId`, `MemoryId`, `ContextHandleId`).\n  - Phase 2 event writer (`EventWriter`) — every workflow call must emit `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, and `PlanCreated` events.\n  - Phase 4 retrieval pipeline (intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidate retrieval, scoring with inclusion reasons).\n  - Phase 5 working-memory operations (`retrieve`, `summarize`, `filter`, `pin`, `evict`, `expand`, `compress`, `checkpoint`).\n  - Phase 7 verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry checks).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references for spec-bound behavior; `AGENTS.md` \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" — legacy tool names continue to respond for a full phase cycle.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 8 and §MCP Tool Contract Principles in full. Copy the bundle field list (\"overview, ranked pivots, relevant context, memory highlights, event episodes, suggested next expansion, stable handles, risks, render choice, structured payload\") into a doc comment on a shared `WorkflowBundle` struct so the schema is self-documenting.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/mod.rs` exporting the four redesigned tools and the shared bundle types (`WorkflowBundle`, `Pivot`, `MemoryHighlight`, `EventEpisode`, `RiskNote`, `ExpansionHint`). Doc-comment the module with a reference to spec §Phase 8.\n3. Implement `prepare_change` in `workflow_v2/prepare_change.rs`: classify intent via the Phase 4 classifier, resolve anchors (paths, symbols, errors, APIs, config keys) into stable identities, run hybrid candidate retrieval across graph + docs + memory + events, apply the Phase 4 scorer, then assemble a `WorkflowBundle` containing code anchors, docs sections, candidate test files (from Phase 4 ranking + `tested_by` edges), memory highlights (each carrying inclusion reason + verification status per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles), risk notes derived from contradiction/supersession state, and verification commands (`cargo test ...`, `npm test`, etc.) inferred from the affected build targets.\n4. Implement `plan_edit` in `workflow_v2/plan_edit.rs`: build on `prepare_change` output and produce a patch-oriented plan with ordered edit steps, each step referencing files/symbols by stable identity, named tests to add or run, docs sections to update (so spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles \"every workflow tool should record inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded high-scoring candidates\" is satisfied).\n5. Implement `trace_scenario` in `workflow_v2/trace_scenario.rs`: walk call chains using the Phase 1 graph between anchor symbols, attaching event-episode evidence where the chain has been exercised before (from event log) and memory annotations describing prior successes/failures on that chain.\n6. Implement `diagnose_failure` in `workflow_v2/diagnose_failure.rs`: take a stack trace, compiler error, or failing test as input, extract anchors, query `FailurePattern` memories and prior `WorkflowFailed` events for similar signatures, surface likely culprit symbols with inclusion reasons + verification status, and suggest a candidate `prepare_change` follow-up bundle handle.\n7. Every tool must emit `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, and `PlanCreated` events via the Phase 2 `EventWriter`. Hot-path budget per spec §Phase 2 DoD: event write must not add more than 5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule`.\n8. Every tool must produce compact rendering by default with stable expansion handles (per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles \"compact rendering, full structured JSON, context handles, stable expansion targets, budget controls, diagnostic explanations where useful\"). The compact form must include enough inclusion reason for an assistant to trust the result.\n9. Register the four `workflow_v2` tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` under their canonical names while keeping the legacy implementations registered with deprecation flags per the T02 compatibility policy.\n10. In `workflow_v2/edit_workflows_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert that `prepare_change` returns a `WorkflowBundle` with at least one ranked pivot, one memory highlight (or an explicit empty rationale), one risk note, and at least one verification command for a fixture repo; (c) assert that no memory with `verification_status = stale | contradicted | superseded | expired | invalidated` appears without an explicit stale label (spec §Phase 7 DoD); (d) assert event-emission order and hot-path budget; (e) assert `plan_edit` produces ordered steps each referencing stable identity types; (f) assert `diagnose_failure` recovers an injected fixture failure pattern.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/prepare_change.rs` — redesigned `prepare_change` composing graph + event + memory + working-memory substrates into a one-call edit-planning bundle per spec §Phase 8.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/plan_edit.rs` — redesigned `plan_edit` producing ordered patch steps with stable-identity anchors, tests, docs updates, and verification commands.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/trace_scenario.rs` — redesigned `trace_scenario` walking call chains and attaching event/memory annotations.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/diagnose_failure.rs` — redesigned `diagnose_failure` matching failures against `FailurePattern` memory and prior `WorkflowFailed` events.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/edit_workflows_tests.rs` — unit + golden tests for each redesigned tool covering schema, stale-label discipline, event emission, hot-path budget, and recovery of fixture failure patterns.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/prepare_change.rs` — confirms the redesigned `prepare_change` file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/plan_edit.rs` — confirms the redesigned `plan_edit` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release` — the new workflow_v2 module compiles cleanly with the rest of the daemon.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::workflow_v2::edit_workflows_tests` — every edit-workflow test passes (schema, stale-label discipline, event emission, hot-path budget, failure-pattern recovery).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split per-tool when needed; do not co-locate two redesigned tools in one file).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `WorkflowBundle` and per-tool request/response structs derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone` and every public field has a doc comment.\n- No silent error handling — every retrieval/verifier/event-writer failure returns a typed error and surfaces in the bundle as a `RiskNote` rather than being swallowed.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (e.g. `MemoryHighlight::inclusion_reason`, not `MemoryHighlight::why_str`).\n- For SQLite: not applicable directly; storage access goes through Phase 2 event writer and Phase 3 memory store APIs, never raw SQL in this module.\n- Observability: every tool emits at least one `tracing::info!` span with `tool`, `task_id`, `session_id`, `bundle_size_bytes`, `event_count` fields per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2` and `## MCP Tool Contract Principles` in module doc comments where behavior is bound by those sections.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every redesigned tool composes graph + event + memory + working-memory substrates in one call; no follow-up \"polish\" task is permitted to add the substrate composition later.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; legacy `prepare_change` / `plan_edit` / `trace_scenario` / `diagnose_failure` continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3831,"cache_read_input_tokens":55400,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3831,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"05c46d50-b298-4f29-a42d-2dbcb0968787","request_id":"req_011Cb7MmdS4mZnDvp53Qjt6x","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2716:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:645:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Ex1LdKtasBCNP4G8NZN2oo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01No1BN99okQNgX1QKKv96qy","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T28.md","content":"# T28 — Phase 4 — Anchor extractor + resolver (paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys)\n\n**Phase:** 4\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R26, T27\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers steps 2 and 3 of the spec's retrieval pipeline: extract every literal anchor a user statement provides — file paths, symbol names, error strings, shell commands, API names, config keys — and resolve each into a stable graph identity using the Phase 1 identity resolver. Anchor resolution is the deterministic core of the pipeline; everything downstream (candidate retrieval, scoring, expansion handles) relies on the resolver returning canonical identities. Ambiguity must be surfaced, not hidden — the resolver returns the candidate set with disambiguation reasons so the scorer in T30 can weigh them and the diagnostic mode in T30/T36 can explain why each candidate was kept.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Pipeline: ... 2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys. 3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: ... anchor extractor and resolver; hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events ...\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n> \"Phase 1: Unified Identity Model — Deliverables: stable IDs for files, symbols, doc sections, events, memories, and follow-up handles; identity resolver with ambiguity diagnostics ...\" (spec §Phase 1: Unified Identity Model)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - R26 verdict — confirms the Phase 3 memory graph (including link tables and identity references) is stable.\n  - T27 `IntentClassification` — anchor extraction is biased by intent (debug tasks prioritize error/stack-trace anchors; refactor tasks prioritize symbol anchors; docs tasks prioritize section/heading anchors).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/` (Phase 1 deliverable; provides the resolver T28 calls).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` (`SymbolId`, file nodes, doc section nodes — the identities resolution targets).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` (T27) — supplies the `IntentClassification` input.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (single source of truth — call the identity resolver, do not reimplement path normalization); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references where behaviour is doc-anchored).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Retrieval Engine pipeline steps 2 and 3, and §Phase 1 identity resolver deliverable, so the inputs and outputs are aligned with the existing identity model.\n2. Design the anchor taxonomy in a doc-comment table at the top of `anchors.rs`: `PathAnchor`, `SymbolAnchor`, `ErrorAnchor` (with optional stack frames), `CommandAnchor`, `ApiAnchor` (HTTP routes, RPC methods, MCP tool names), `ConfigKeyAnchor`. Each variant declares the regex/token shape it matches and the identity family it resolves to.\n3. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/anchors.rs`: pure-function extractor `extract_anchors(task_text: &str, intent: &IntentClassification) -> Vec<RawAnchor>` and resolver `resolve_anchors(raws: Vec<RawAnchor>, resolver: &IdentityResolver) -> Vec<ResolvedAnchor>`. Resolution returns the canonical identity, the candidate set when ambiguous, the ambiguity reason, and the source span in the task text.\n4. Route every path normalization, symbol lookup, and doc-section lookup through the existing Phase 1 `IdentityResolver` API — do not reimplement these in `anchors.rs` (single source of truth per `coding.md`).\n5. Bias extraction by intent: for `debug` intent, raise the error/stack-frame regex priority and accept commonly-occurring error idioms (`thread 'X' panicked at ...`, `error[E\\d+]:`, `Traceback (most recent call last):`); for `refactor`/`add_feature`/`modify_feature`, raise symbol-name extraction; for `update_docs`, raise Markdown heading and `path/to/file.md#heading` anchor extraction. Document the bias rules inline with `## Retrieval Engine` heading citation.\n6. Emit a `diagnostics()` view on `ResolvedAnchor` returning `(anchor_text, resolved_identity, ambiguity_reason, source_span)` so the scorer (T30) can apply `exact identifier match` weighting and the diagnostic mode can show why an anchor was matched or skipped.\n7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/anchors_tests.rs` covering: each anchor variant detected in canonical inputs; intent bias rerouting (debug inputs surface errors first); ambiguous symbol names return the full candidate set with reason; unknown paths resolve to a `PathAnchor::Unresolved` rather than panicking; resolver round-trip preserves source spans; serde round-trip for the diagnostics view; empty input returns an empty vector without panic.\n8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/anchors.rs` — anchor taxonomy, extractor, resolver, diagnostics view.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/anchors_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for extraction, resolution, intent bias, ambiguity, and diagnostics.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/anchors.rs` — confirms the extractor/resolver module exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::anchors_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every anchor variant, intent bias, and ambiguity case is covered.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Extraction is table-driven (regex/feature table) rather than nested match blocks.\n- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: identity resolution is delegated to the Phase 1 `IdentityResolver`; no duplicated path/symbol normalization in this module.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent.\n- Doc citation: comments describing behaviour mandated by `## 7. Retrieval Engine` or `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` cite those exact headings per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every anchor type in spec §Retrieval Engine step 2 — paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys — has a variant, extractor, resolver path, and test.\n- [ ] All resolution paths call the Phase 1 `IdentityResolver` (no parallel implementation).\n- [ ] Diagnostics view is exported and exercised by a test, ready to be consumed by T29 (candidates), T30 (scoring), and T36 (working-memory inspector).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3305,"cache_read_input_tokens":46146,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3305,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"bd1ae9ee-9ddd-4c07-85d0-25be489b433d","request_id":"req_011Cb7MofXP1UUJjGA88Hkuu","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2717:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:651:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RsNa6ebsnWVeUhpsNvGQen","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T06.md","content":"# T06 — Phase 1 — Identity type system (File, Symbol, Doc, Section, Event, Memory, Handle)\n\n**Phase:** 1\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R05\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the unified identity type system that every later substrate (event log, memory graph, working memory, retrieval, verification) depends on. Spec §Phase 1 states \"Identity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation.\" The task delivers stable, encodable, decodable, hashable id types for each of the seven identity kinds plus the encoding/decoding rules that make them compact in MCP payloads. The existing `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` is generalized into a common identity model.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 1 Deliverables: stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles; identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs; explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads; compatibility support for legacy name-based references; tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity; ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches; identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R05 review (validates Phase 0 artifacts) and the layout decisions in `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-crate-boundary-plan.md` (decides whether identity is a `lattice-identity` crate or a `lattice-core::identity` module).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` (current node identifier types)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` (current `SymbolId` usage in `expand_context`)\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md` (existing handle conventions to honor and generalize)\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), naming (intent over mechanism), single source of truth (replace ad-hoc id strings, do not fork them); `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility (legacy `SymbolId` callers must keep compiling via a re-export or `From` impl).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read the crate boundary plan from T03 to confirm whether identity lives in `daemon/crates/lattice-identity/` or `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/`. The files_expected list assumes the in-`lattice-core` module layout; if T03 chose a separate crate, mirror the file structure under that crate and update the workspace `Cargo.toml`.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` declaring the `kinds`, `encoding`, and `tests` submodules and re-exporting the public identity types.\n3. In `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs`, define one struct per identity kind with the exact fields required by the spec node/event/memory model:\n   - `FileId { workspace_id, repo_relative_path, content_hash }`\n   - `SymbolId { file: FileId, qualified_name, byte_offset, kind }` (generalizing the existing `SymbolId`)\n   - `DocId { workspace_id, repo_relative_path, content_hash }`\n   - `SectionId { doc: DocId, heading_path: Vec<String>, byte_offset }`\n   - `EventId { workspace_id, ulid }` (ULID for monotonic ordering)\n   - `MemoryId { workspace_id, ulid }`\n   - `ContextHandleId { workspace_id, session_id, ulid }`\n4. Each id type derives `Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize`. Implement `Display` to produce the compact MCP wire form.\n5. Define a common `IdentityKind` enum and an `Identity` enum that wraps the seven kinds so any subsystem can hold a heterogeneous identity reference.\n6. In `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/encoding.rs`, implement compact wire encoding rules: a stable string form (`file:<workspace>/<path>@<hash>`, `symbol:<file>#<qname>@<offset>`, `event:<workspace>/<ulid>`, etc.) and a decoder that round-trips. Reject malformed strings with a typed `IdentityDecodeError`.\n7. Provide `From<old_SymbolId> for SymbolId` and a re-export under the prior path so existing callers continue compiling.\n8. In `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/tests.rs`, write unit tests for: round-trip encoding for every kind; equality semantics; hash stability across process restarts (deterministic for same inputs); `IdentityDecodeError` cases (empty, missing prefix, malformed hash); `From<old_SymbolId>` migration.\n9. Add the module to `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/lib.rs` with `pub mod identity;` and document the substrate in a module-level doc comment that cites the spec heading.\n10. Run `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` and `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::tests` to confirm green.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` — module entrypoint, re-exports, module docs citing spec §Phase 1.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs` — the seven id structs, `Identity` enum, `IdentityKind` enum.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/encoding.rs` — compact wire form encode/decode with `IdentityDecodeError`.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/tests.rs` — unit tests covering round-trip, equality, hashing, decode errors, and the legacy `SymbolId` migration shim.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` — confirms the module file exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs` — confirms the kinds file exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` — confirms the crate compiles with the new module integrated.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::tests` — confirms all identity unit tests pass.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (verify with `wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/*.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — applies to every encode/decode helper.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions; if `#[allow(clippy::...)]` is required, justify on the same line.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `IdentityDecodeError`).\n- For schema changes: not applicable (no SQL schema in this task); type-level schema parity is asserted via the `Identity` enum covering all seven kinds with no orphan fields.\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` heading from the spec in `mod.rs`; reference `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md` headings rather than duplicating prior handle behavior (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 1 punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"36cefa05-6830-4681-aa11-def156e8cd29","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2718:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:660:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VyFsJMQCiudocXkZ3sZYhd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T07.md","content":"# T07 — Phase 1 — Identity resolver with ambiguity diagnostics\n\n**Phase:** 1\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T06\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task implements the resolver that turns user-supplied or LLM-supplied references — paths, qualified symbol names, doc headings, test names, event refs — into the stable `Identity` values defined in T06. Spec §Phase 1 names \"identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\" as a deliverable and binds the resolver to return ambiguity diagnostics rather than arbitrary matches when an input has multiple candidate identities. Without this resolver, every later phase would re-implement name→id lookups inconsistently, and ambiguity would be silently swallowed.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 1 Deliverables: ... identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs ... compatibility support for legacy name-based references.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity; ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: identity types from T06 (`daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs`, `encoding.rs`).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` (workspace graph that backs path/symbol lookups)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` (SQLite-backed lookups for files, symbols, docs, sections)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/parser/` (doc heading and symbol extraction the resolver consumes)\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` hard limits, naming (intent over mechanism), error handling (resolver errors must be structured, never silent); `AGENTS.md` (resolver public API is binding once shipped — any change is a contract change).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs` with a `pub struct IdentityResolver` that holds references to the graph, storage, and parser indexes needed to answer lookups.\n2. Define the resolver public API:\n   - `resolve_path(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, path: &str) -> Result<FileId, ResolveError>`\n   - `resolve_symbol(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, qualified_name: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SymbolId>`\n   - `resolve_section(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, doc: &DocId, heading: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SectionId>`\n   - `resolve_test(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, test_name: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SymbolId>` (tests are identified as symbols with a `Test` kind)\n   - `resolve_event_ref(&self, workspace: &WorkspaceId, ref_str: &str) -> Result<EventId, ResolveError>`\n3. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/ambiguity.rs` with the `ResolveOutcome<T>` enum (`Unique(T)`, `Ambiguous(AmbiguityReport<T>)`, `NotFound(ResolveError)`) and the `AmbiguityReport<T>` struct carrying the list of candidates plus a `disambiguation_hint` describing how the caller can narrow the query (suggest adding parent module, file path, byte offset, etc.).\n4. The resolver must never pick arbitrarily when multiple matches exist — return `Ambiguous`. Tests in T09 will assert this on duplicate symbol names.\n5. Add a legacy compatibility helper `pub fn resolve_legacy_symbol_name(&self, name: &str) -> ResolveOutcome<SymbolId>` that takes a name without workspace context and routes to the resolver via the default workspace, preserving older MCP payloads.\n6. Implement caching: keep a small LRU keyed by `(workspace, query)` so the same resolution within a session does not re-traverse the graph. Cache hits must still preserve ambiguity semantics — a cached `Ambiguous` returns `Ambiguous`.\n7. Errors are structured: `ResolveError::WorkspaceNotIndexed`, `ResolveError::Malformed`, `ResolveError::IndexLagBehind { latest_event_id }`. No bare `String` errors.\n8. Add module-level docs in both files citing spec §Phase 1.\n9. Register the new submodules in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs` with `pub mod resolver;` and `pub mod ambiguity;`.\n10. Write smoke unit tests inline (the full ambiguity / rename / branch-change test matrix is owned by T09): construct a tiny resolver fixture and assert `resolve_path` returns a `FileId`, `resolve_symbol` on a duplicate name returns `Ambiguous`, and `resolve_legacy_symbol_name` falls back correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs` — `IdentityResolver` struct, the five `resolve_*` methods, LRU cache, structured `ResolveError`.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/ambiguity.rs` — `ResolveOutcome<T>`, `AmbiguityReport<T>`, `disambiguation_hint` helpers.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs` — confirms the resolver file exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` — confirms the crate compiles with the resolver and ambiguity modules wired into `lattice-core`.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::resolver` — confirms the resolver smoke tests pass.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::ambiguity` — confirms the ambiguity module tests pass.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (verify with `wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver.rs daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/ambiguity.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — every `resolve_*` method must fit; extract candidate-collection helpers if needed.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`resolve_symbol` not `lookup`, `AmbiguityReport` not `MultiResult`).\n- For schema changes: not applicable (resolver consumes existing storage).\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` heading from the spec in module docs; if any non-obvious caching or LRU choice is made, cite the heading from `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md` that authorizes the behavior (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 1 punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"1a20f582-7bfb-4d3c-8dba-826aa42e26d0","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2719:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:666:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01PNvKdQfDjd2TySTG7S6XBT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01764msZGhk1gzVnVQcgp6n3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T29.md","content":"# T29 — Phase 4 — Hybrid candidate retrieval (graph + docs + memories + events + working set)\n\n**Phase:** 4\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T27, T28\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers pipeline steps 4 through 7 from spec §Retrieval Engine: gather candidate results from every source enumerated under \"Candidate sources\" — exact path/symbol lookup, code graph traversal, doc backlinks/outgoing links, FTS, embeddings, event similarity, memory links, workflow similarity, recent active working memory — and produce a unified `Candidate` stream the scorer (T30) can rank. Each candidate carries its source, the resolved anchor(s) that produced it, and a preliminary `inclusion_reason` string the shaper (T31) will refine. Hot-path budgets from spec §Phase 1/§Phase 2 still apply, so per-source retrieval is bounded by configurable caps and never performs unbounded traversal.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Pipeline: ... 4. Retrieve graph candidates. 5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams. 6. Retrieve relevant event episodes. 7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Candidate sources: exact path/symbol lookup; code graph traversal; doc backlinks/outgoing links; FTS; embeddings; event similarity; memory links; workflow similarity; recent active working memory.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: ... hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events ...\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n> \"No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\" (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T27 `IntentClassification` — selects which candidate sources are weighted/required for the active intent.\n  - T28 `ResolvedAnchor` stream — provides the seed identities for exact lookup, graph traversal, and doc backlink expansion.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` — graph traversal entry points.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — FTS (`memories_fts`, planned doc FTS) and embedding index access.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/embeddings/` and `.lattice/vector_index/` USearch handle — embedding nearest-neighbour lookup.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` (Phase 2 deliverable) — event similarity / episode lookup APIs.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — typed memory streams + link tables (Phase 3 deliverables).\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, single source of truth — reuse existing search APIs); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (no broken windows; hot-path budgets are explicit).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Retrieval Engine \"Candidate sources\" and \"Pipeline\" steps 4–7 in full; note the explicit ordering between exact lookup → graph traversal → docs → FTS/embeddings → events → memory links → workflow → working memory.\n2. Define `Candidate { identity, source, seed_anchor, raw_score, preliminary_inclusion_reason, expansion_handle_hint }` in `candidates.rs`. The `source` variant enumerates exactly the nine spec-named sources; no additional sources sneak in without spec amendment.\n3. Implement one async retriever per source as a small dedicated function (≤50 lines each), gated by a per-source budget cap drawn from a `RetrievalBudget` struct (max candidates per source, max graph hops, max FTS rows, embedding k, event window, working-memory window). Defaults must come from configuration, not literals scattered across functions.\n4. Compose the retrievers in `retrieve_candidates(intent: &IntentClassification, anchors: &[ResolvedAnchor], budget: &RetrievalBudget, ctx: &RetrievalContext) -> Vec<Candidate>` so the orchestration is one place, parallel via `tokio::join!` where safe, and short-circuits when intent disqualifies a source (e.g. `update_docs` skips event similarity unless explicitly requested).\n5. Wire bounded graph expansion (spec pipeline step 7) through the existing graph-traversal API — never call `petgraph` directly here. Expansion radius is `min(intent_default, budget.max_graph_hops)` and every traversal must record the path that produced the candidate (used by T30 graph-proximity scoring and by T31's expansion handle).\n6. Hot-path discipline: log the candidate count per source and total elapsed time at `debug` (never `info` on hot paths). Assert in debug builds that no single retriever exceeded its budget cap; in release builds, truncate with a `BudgetExhausted` flag on the candidate set so the scorer/shaper can surface it.\n7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates_tests.rs` covering: each of the nine sources produces candidates for an appropriate seed; budgets are enforced (synthetic huge graph does not blow up); intent gating skips disqualified sources; bounded expansion records traversal paths; FTS, embeddings, and event similarity all participate when present; working-memory window returns the most-recent N items from the session context (interface stub acceptable here — T35 wires the real producer); empty anchor list still yields working-memory and event-similarity results when those exist.\n8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates.rs` — `Candidate` type, per-source retrievers, orchestrator, budget enforcement.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for source coverage, budgets, intent gating, bounded expansion, traversal-path recording.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates.rs` — confirms the candidates module exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::candidates_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every spec-named source has at least one passing test.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. If `candidates.rs` approaches the cap, split per-source retrievers into `candidates/` submodules along source boundaries — never along arbitrary line counts.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Each retriever is its own function; the orchestrator is composition, not a giant match.\n- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: graph traversal, FTS, and embedding queries are issued through their existing module APIs; no duplicated SQL or vector-index calls in this module.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; hot-path log levels are correct (`debug`, not `info`).\n- Doc citation: comments naming the spec-defined source list cite `## 7. Retrieval Engine` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the nine spec-named candidate sources has a retriever and a test.\n- [ ] All retrievals are budget-bounded; no unbounded traversal or FTS scan on the hot path.\n- [ ] Candidates expose `source`, `seed_anchor`, and `expansion_handle_hint`, ready to be consumed by T30 (scoring) and T31 (shaper).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3289,"cache_read_input_tokens":49451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3289,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"7df1eee1-94df-4033-8589-97a6debfac05","request_id":"req_011Cb7MrqhtXNNiGHiH8xTwy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2720:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:669:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01QHgJUDU2Z1BZn1GKKokHZ6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012eScWFNCfoznDfAaZ3YQCU","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T57.md","content":"# T57 — Phase 8 — Redesigned get_context_capsule + get_docs_capsule + find_relevant_tests + impact_from_diff\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-8-workflow-engine-v2)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task redesigns the four discovery workflow tools (`get_context_capsule`, `get_docs_capsule`, `find_relevant_tests`, `impact_from_diff`) on top of the Phase 1–7 substrates so that each tool returns a compact bundle by default with deliberate expansion handles, rather than dumping broad slices of files. Spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties requires \"every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles\" and spec §Phase 8 DoD requires workflows that \"produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping.\" Without this redesign, discovery calls remain the dominant tool-call cost in Phase 9 metrics and the success criterion \"40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\" cannot be met.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\" (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties)\n\n> \"Phase 8 Deliverables: redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation; workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates.\"\n\n> \"Every tool response should support: compact rendering, full structured JSON, context handles, stable expansion targets, budget controls, diagnostic explanations where useful.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Definition of done: common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls; workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — verification statuses must be surfaced inside every discovery bundle so stale memory cannot appear as trusted guidance.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/context_capsule.rs` — current heuristic context capsule, retained as a compatibility shim per the T02 compatibility policy.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/docs_capsule.rs` — current docs capsule.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/find_relevant_tests.rs` — current relevant-test discovery.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/impact_from_diff.rs` — current diff-based impact analysis.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/context_cache.rs` — context-handle cache, reused for stable expansion targets.\n  - Phase 1 identity types; Phase 2 event writer; Phase 4 retrieval pipeline (intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidate retrieval, compact response shaper); Phase 7 verification engine.\n  - T56 shared `WorkflowBundle`, `Pivot`, `MemoryHighlight`, `EventEpisode`, `RiskNote`, `ExpansionHint` types.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references; `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 8 and §MCP Tool Contract Principles in full. Reuse the shared bundle types from T56 (`WorkflowBundle`, `Pivot`, `MemoryHighlight`, `EventEpisode`, `RiskNote`, `ExpansionHint`) so all eight Phase 8 tools share one schema surface; do not duplicate the structs in `workflow_v2/`.\n2. Implement `get_context_capsule` in `workflow_v2/context_capsule.rs`: take a free-form task or file/symbol anchor list, run the Phase 4 intent classifier + anchor resolver, retrieve compact candidates from graph + memory + docs + events, score them, and produce a compact bundle with at most N pivots (default 8, configurable via budget control). Each pivot must carry an inclusion reason and a stable expansion handle. Support `mode: \"focused\" | \"compact\" | \"full\" | \"diagnostic\"` per spec §MCP Surface render modes.\n3. Implement `get_docs_capsule` in `workflow_v2/docs_capsule.rs`: anchor on doc sections rather than code symbols, traverse `documents` and `mentions` edges to surface adjacent prose and ADRs, and include code anchors that the cited sections reference. Output bundle must distinguish \"doc primary\" pivots from \"code-referenced\" pivots so the consumer knows where the authoritative text lives.\n4. Implement `find_relevant_tests` in `workflow_v2/relevant_tests.rs`: accept either a file/symbol set or a diff, traverse `tested_by` edges in the Phase 1 graph, combine with `co_changed_with` evidence from the event log, score by graph proximity + past test-failure frequency, and return ranked test files with the verification command needed to run each (e.g. `cargo test -p X --lib mod::tests::test_name`). Spec §Measurable Success Criteria targets 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks — the API must record per-recommendation provenance so T69 regression tests can measure recall.\n5. Implement `impact_from_diff` in `workflow_v2/impact_from_diff.rs`: accept a diff or list of changed files/symbols, walk dependent edges (`calls`, `imports`, `implements`, `extends`, `type_ref`, `depends_on`) up to a bounded depth (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties forbids unbounded traversal on hot paths), attach affected tests via `tested_by`, attach affected docs via `documents`, and surface high-impact warnings as `RiskNote`s.\n6. Each tool must emit `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ContextBundleReturned`, and `MemoryRetrieved` events via the Phase 2 `EventWriter`. Hot-path budget per spec §Phase 2 DoD: ≤5ms P99 event-write overhead on `get_context_capsule`.\n7. Each tool must register a stable context handle in `rpc/context_cache.rs` so `expand_context` can fetch the next delta cheaply per spec §MCP Surface \"stable expansion targets\" and spec §First Implementation Slice \"Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\"\n8. Register the four `workflow_v2` tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`; keep legacy implementations registered with deprecation flags per the T02 compatibility policy.\n9. In `workflow_v2/discovery_workflows_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert default render mode is compact and bundle size stays under the configured budget; (c) assert `get_context_capsule` returns expansion handles that `expand_context` can dereference; (d) assert `find_relevant_tests` recovers known golden tests for fixture repos; (e) assert `impact_from_diff` bounded-traversal cap is enforced; (f) assert no stale/contradicted memory appears without a stale label.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/context_capsule.rs` — redesigned `get_context_capsule` with intent-aware compact bundles and render-mode support.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/docs_capsule.rs` — redesigned `get_docs_capsule` distinguishing doc-primary and code-referenced pivots.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/relevant_tests.rs` — redesigned `find_relevant_tests` returning ranked tests with verification commands and per-recommendation provenance for T69 metric tests.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/impact_from_diff.rs` — redesigned `impact_from_diff` with bounded traversal, affected-test attachment, and high-impact `RiskNote`s.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/discovery_workflows_tests.rs` — unit + golden tests covering schema, compact-by-default, handle round-trip, fixture recovery, bounded traversal, and stale-label discipline.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/context_capsule.rs` — confirms the redesigned `get_context_capsule` exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/impact_from_diff.rs` — confirms the redesigned `impact_from_diff` exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::workflow_v2::discovery_workflows_tests` — every discovery-workflow test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split per-tool when needed).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: reuse the shared `WorkflowBundle` schema from T56; do not introduce a parallel response type unless explicitly justified.\n- No silent error handling — every retrieval/traversal/event-writer failure returns a typed error and surfaces as a `RiskNote` rather than being swallowed.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`ContextCapsuleRequest::render_mode`, not `ContextCapsuleRequest::mode_str`).\n- For SQLite: not applicable directly; storage access goes through Phase 2/3/7 APIs.\n- Observability: each tool emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `task_id`, `session_id`, `bundle_size_bytes`, `pivot_count`, `handle_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2`, `## MCP Surface`, and `## Non-Negotiable Product Properties` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: compact-by-default + expansion handles + render modes are all implemented in this task; no \"later optimization\" is permitted to ship them later.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; legacy `get_context_capsule` / `get_docs_capsule` / `find_relevant_tests` / `impact_from_diff` continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4964,"cache_read_input_tokens":59231,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4964,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"1fcd67dc-db59-4f9e-ba4f-c866f1a722c9","request_id":"req_011Cb7MrQcmeoxoHC3sgQkGJ","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2721:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:672:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016wpFR2N3dUb7fdjiDggmL3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T08.md","content":"# T08 — Phase 1 — Identity serialization in MCP payloads + legacy name compatibility shim\n\n**Phase:** 1\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T07\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task wires the new `Identity` types into the MCP JSON-RPC surface so every workflow tool response carries stable identities the client can expand later, while preserving legacy name-based fields so existing clients continue to function. Spec §Phase 1 deliverables include \"explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\" and \"compatibility support for legacy name-based references\". The compatibility shim is the binding mechanism by which T02's MCP compatibility policy is honored for Phase 1.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 1 Deliverables: ... explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads; compatibility support for legacy name-based references.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T06 identity types (`daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/kinds.rs`, `encoding.rs`).\n  - T07 resolver and ambiguity modules (`resolver.rs`, `ambiguity.rs`).\n  - T02 compatibility policy (`docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`) — binding for which tools must add additive identity fields vs. shimmed legacy fields.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` (current MCP dispatch and payload shapes)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` (JSON-RPC server)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/context_cache.rs` (handle cache that must store new `ContextHandleId`)\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (no broken windows, single source of truth, schema parity), `AGENTS.md` (binding compatibility shim — every legacy field stays populated for one phase cycle).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` with serde helpers that emit the compact wire form defined in T06 `encoding.rs` and accept either compact form or structured object form on deserialize.\n2. Define a `pub struct IdentityPayload` containing both the compact `id` string and a structured `fields` object, plus an optional `legacy_name` field for backward compatibility. Serialization round-trips through both forms.\n3. Add `pub fn serialize_outcome<T: Into<Identity>>(outcome: ResolveOutcome<T>) -> IdentityPayloadOrAmbiguity` that converts the resolver outcome to the wire shape, preserving `Ambiguous` as a structured diagnostic instead of an arbitrary pick.\n4. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` with the MCP wiring: a wrapper that decorates every workflow tool response (or its top-level pivot list) with `IdentityPayload` values and preserves the legacy name field listed in the T02 compatibility policy for each tool.\n5. Wire one representative tool (`get_context_capsule` or `prepare_change`) end-to-end through the new serialization wrapper as a proof of integration; full Phase 8 redesign of every tool is owned later, but Phase 1 must demonstrate the integration path works without regressing existing clients.\n6. Add tests under `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests`) covering: compact-form round-trip, structured-form acceptance, legacy-name preservation, ambiguity payload shape.\n7. Add tests under `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` covering: a full tool response that includes both identity payloads and legacy fields, and a response where the resolver returned `Ambiguous` (the response must surface the ambiguity diagnostic rather than silently picking).\n8. Update the workflow tool that was wired in step 5 to emit identity payloads alongside its prior fields; document the change in `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` under the per-tool classification.\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release`, `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- identity::serialization`, and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- rpc::identity_payload` to confirm green.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` — `IdentityPayload`, serde helpers, `serialize_outcome`, inline tests.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` — MCP wrapper that decorates workflow responses with identity payloads and preserves legacy name fields.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs` — confirms the serialization module exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs` — confirms the RPC integration module exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release` — confirms the full workspace builds in release profile with the new modules wired in.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- identity::serialization` — confirms the serialization tests pass.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- rpc::identity_payload` — confirms the RPC integration tests pass.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (verify with `wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/serialization.rs daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/identity_payload.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — applies to every serde helper and wrapper.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`IdentityPayload`, `serialize_outcome`, not `IdData` or `to_json`).\n- For schema changes: not applicable (no SQL schema added); JSON schema parity is asserted by tests covering both legacy and new fields.\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` heading from the spec and the per-tool classification heading from `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` for every additive field introduced (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 1 punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"f2172cba-383d-413f-b510-a7ed9826dfd5","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2722:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:684:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012ZX7F3AyQWmYbR3Eht2XW9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VnrPpS4EXKui27kxGWfrbU","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T30.md","content":"# T30 — Phase 4 — Scoring model with diagnostic mode (all required signals)\n\n**Phase:** 4\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T29\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Ranking Complexity](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#ranking-complexity)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers pipeline step 8 — the scorer — and the diagnostic mode that makes the scorer auditable per spec §Ranking Complexity. The scorer must apply every ranking signal the spec enumerates, expose each signal as a named, inspectable feature, and emit both scores and human-readable reasons under a diagnostic mode. Compact mode emits only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result; diagnostic mode emits the full per-signal breakdown. The scorer must remain feature-based — no learned policy is acceptable in Phase 4 — so retrieval ranking stays explainable for the lifetime of this phase.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Ranking signals: task-type compatibility; graph proximity to anchors; exact identifier match; semantic similarity; verification status; freshness; scope; evidence strength; contradiction/supersession state; past usefulness; recent successful reuse; user preference compatibility; token cost.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"The ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: ... scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness ... diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons ...\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n> \"Risk: ranking becomes hard to reason about. Control: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\" (spec §Ranking Complexity)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T29 `Candidate` stream — provides candidates with source, seed anchor, traversal path, and preliminary inclusion reason.\n  - T27 `IntentClassification` — drives `task-type compatibility`.\n  - T28 `ResolvedAnchor` set — drives `exact identifier match` and `graph proximity to anchors`.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — provides `verification status`, `freshness`, `scope`, `evidence strength`, `contradiction/supersession state` fields per the Phase 3 schema.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` (Phase 2) — provides `past usefulness` and `recent successful reuse` via memory-access events and `WorkflowSucceeded`/`MemoryRetrieved`.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/embeddings/` — provides `semantic similarity` (cosine) for the candidate's text against the anchor/intent text.\n  - User-preference store from Phase 3 (memory of `UserPreferenceObserved`) — drives `user preference compatibility`.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, table-driven scoring); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references in code comments where behaviour is doc-anchored).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Retrieval Engine \"Ranking signals\" and §Ranking Complexity in full; copy the thirteen-signal list verbatim into a doc-comment table at the top of `scoring.rs`.\n2. Define the data structures: `SignalScore { signal: SignalKind, raw: f32, weighted: f32, reason: String }`, `RankedCandidate { candidate: Candidate, total_score: f32, signal_scores: Vec<SignalScore>, inclusion_reason: String }`, `ScoringWeights` (one weight per signal, defaulting from a documented `RetrievalProfile`), and `DiagnosticMode { Compact, Diagnostic }`.\n3. Implement one pure-function evaluator per signal in `scoring.rs`, each ≤50 lines, each returning a `SignalScore` with both score and reason. The thirteen evaluators are: `task_type_compatibility`, `graph_proximity`, `exact_identifier_match`, `semantic_similarity`, `verification_status`, `freshness`, `scope`, `evidence_strength`, `contradiction_or_supersession_state`, `past_usefulness`, `recent_successful_reuse`, `user_preference_compatibility`, `token_cost`. Each MUST be present; missing data yields a score of 0 and a reason like `\"no <field> available; signal skipped\"`, never a panic or silent skip.\n4. Implement `score_candidates(candidates: Vec<Candidate>, intent: &IntentClassification, anchors: &[ResolvedAnchor], weights: &ScoringWeights, mode: DiagnosticMode, ctx: &ScoringContext) -> Vec<RankedCandidate>` that calls each evaluator, sums weighted scores, fills `inclusion_reason` with a compact summary in `Compact` mode and the full per-signal list in `Diagnostic` mode, then sorts descending.\n5. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs`: the public API for diagnostic mode. Exposes `RankingDiagnostics { intent, anchors, candidates_per_source, ranked: Vec<RankedCandidate>, weights, budget_exhaustion_flags }` plus serde derivation, so T31 (shaper) and T36 (MCP working-memory inspector) can hand the diagnostics straight to the caller without further transformation.\n6. Honor spec §Stale Memory Leakage by ensuring `verification_status` and `contradiction_or_supersession_state` apply a hard penalty (not just a soft weight) when status is `stale`, `contradicted`, or `superseded`, so these candidates cannot rank above trusted ones except when diagnostics explicitly request them. The penalty value and policy live in `ScoringWeights` with inline documentation citing `## Stale Memory Leakage`.\n7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring_tests.rs` covering: each of the thirteen signals is evaluated for a representative candidate; signal absence yields a `0.0` score and a documented reason (no panic); stale/contradicted candidates rank below trusted candidates with the same raw match; diagnostic mode round-trips signal scores and reasons through serde; compact mode produces a non-empty single-line inclusion reason; weight overrides change the ranking deterministically; user-preference compatibility raises the score of candidates matching a stored preference and lowers candidates conflicting with one.\n8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring.rs` — signal evaluators, weights, orchestrator.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs` — diagnostics types and serde, public API for diagnostic mode.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for every signal, both modes, and the stale-memory hard penalty.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring.rs` — confirms the scorer module exists at the expected path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs` — confirms the diagnostics module exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::scoring_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every signal and both modes are covered.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. If `scoring.rs` approaches the cap, split signal evaluators into `scoring/` submodules grouped by data dependency (graph signals, memory signals, history signals).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Each signal evaluator is its own function.\n- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task, but field reads from `memories` use the canonical structs).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: weights, defaults, and signal-kind enum are defined once and consumed everywhere; no parallel weight tables in callers.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; no silent `.unwrap()` on Optional signal data.\n- Doc citation: the signal table at the top of `scoring.rs` cites `## 7. Retrieval Engine`; the stale-memory hard penalty comment cites `## Stale Memory Leakage` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the thirteen ranking signals from spec §Retrieval Engine is implemented as a signal evaluator with a test.\n- [ ] Diagnostic mode emits both per-signal scores and reasons; compact mode emits a single-line inclusion reason — both verified by tests.\n- [ ] Stale/contradicted/superseded candidates cannot rank above trusted candidates with the same raw match (regression test pinned).\n- [ ] Diagnostics struct is exported and exercised by a test, ready to be consumed by T31 (shaper) and T36 (working-memory inspector).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3306,"cache_read_input_tokens":52740,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3306,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":58,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"51d549ac-52ac-4ed7-9cd1-26fc808d3401","request_id":"req_011Cb7MvMPAgod1NwX41bo3F","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2723:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:687:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HjWPSEuRpn3Jo5PGV1v96r","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T09.md","content":"# T09 — Phase 1 — Identity resolution tests (renames, moves, duplicate names, branch changes, P99 budget)\n\n**Phase:** 1\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T06, T07, T08\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 1: Unified Identity Model](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-1-unified-identity-model)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the full Phase 1 test matrix that the spec enumerates and the hot-path budget evidence that R10 requires. Spec §Phase 1 explicitly lists \"tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\" and the DoD states \"identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\". This task creates two test files: `resolver_tests.rs` (correctness matrix) and `budget_tests.rs` (P99 measurement under an `#[ignore]` gate so a normal `cargo test` is fast and the slow budget tests run on the review pass).\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 1 Deliverables: ... tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: ... identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T06 identity types.\n  - T07 resolver and ambiguity modules.\n  - T08 serialization and RPC wiring (budget tests measure resolver calls as they are exercised through the same paths the MCP tools take).\n  - T04 baseline metrics (`docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/baselines/baseline_metrics.json`) — budget tests assert the delta vs. baseline.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` (fixture conventions to mirror)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/parser/` (used to construct realistic fixtures with multiple symbols sharing a name)\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (one test per behavior, test reads like a spec, no fixtures larger than the assertion), `AGENTS.md` (do not regress baseline timings while measuring).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver_tests.rs` covering the spec test matrix. One test per behavior:\n   - `test_resolve_path_unique_returns_fileid`\n   - `test_resolve_path_after_file_rename_returns_new_id`\n   - `test_resolve_path_after_file_rename_legacy_name_returns_via_compat_shim`\n   - `test_resolve_symbol_unique_returns_symbolid`\n   - `test_resolve_symbol_duplicate_name_returns_ambiguous_with_disambiguation_hint`\n   - `test_resolve_section_after_heading_rename_returns_new_id`\n   - `test_resolve_section_after_section_move_returns_new_id`\n   - `test_resolve_symbol_on_different_branch_returns_branch_scoped_id`\n   - `test_resolve_event_ref_round_trips_through_encoding`\n   - `test_resolve_legacy_symbol_name_routes_via_default_workspace`\n2. Each test constructs a minimal in-memory or tempdir fixture using the existing parser to populate a tiny graph; assertions verify the exact `ResolveOutcome` variant and the disambiguation hint content.\n3. Add a `helpers` submodule (inline) with a fixture builder that other identity tests can reuse — single source of truth for fixture construction.\n4. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/budget_tests.rs` with the P99 measurement. Each budget test is marked `#[ignore]` so a default `cargo test` stays fast and the review runs them with `-- --include-ignored`.\n   - `test_resolve_symbol_p99_within_2ms_budget` — runs 10,000 lookups, computes p99, asserts ≤2ms.\n   - `test_resolve_path_p99_within_2ms_budget`\n   - `test_resolve_section_p99_within_2ms_budget`\n   - `test_resolver_does_not_regress_baseline_workflow_p99` — exercises the resolver through the same hot path as `prepare_change` and asserts the delta vs. `baseline_metrics.json` is ≤2ms.\n5. Each budget test loads `baseline_metrics.json` from the fixed path, computes the new p99, and asserts the delta with a clear failure message naming the baseline number and the observed number.\n6. Add module declarations in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/mod.rs`: `#[cfg(test)] mod resolver_tests;` and `#[cfg(test)] mod budget_tests;`.\n7. Run `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::resolver_tests` (fast tests) and `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::budget_tests -- --include-ignored` (budget tests) to confirm green.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver_tests.rs` — full correctness matrix for renames, moves, duplicates, branch changes, legacy compat.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/budget_tests.rs` — `#[ignore]`-gated P99 measurement tests asserting ≤2ms resolver budget and no regression vs. baseline.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver_tests.rs` — confirms the resolver test file exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/budget_tests.rs` — confirms the budget test file exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::resolver_tests` — confirms the correctness matrix passes; every named test must pass.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib identity::budget_tests -- --include-ignored` — confirms the budget tests pass with the `#[ignore]` gate lifted; passing means the resolver is within the 2ms P99 budget defined in spec §Phase 1 DoD.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (verify with `wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/resolver_tests.rs daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/budget_tests.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — applies to every test function and fixture helper.\n- Test length ≤30 lines per `coding.md` test discipline — extract fixture builders into the `helpers` submodule when setup grows.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming — test names read as behavior specs (`test_resolve_symbol_duplicate_name_returns_ambiguous_with_disambiguation_hint`, not `test_resolve_1`).\n- For schema changes: not applicable.\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Phase 1: Unified Identity Model` heading from the spec in a module-level doc comment naming the four spec-mandated test categories (renames, moves, duplicate names, branch changes) and the 2ms P99 budget.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 1 punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"f2b3564e-afa4-4acf-b894-9e924f75df5a","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2724:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:689:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01CqnMhG1wN36C2yteTWR1rZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FtML53V5rSKF5hu5wzzt4s","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T58.md","content":"# T58 — Phase 8 — New memory tools: get_task_memory + save_memory + propose_memory_evolution(action=apply|reject)\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces three new memory-facing MCP tools required by spec §MCP Surface: `get_task_memory` (read working memory + relevant durable memory for the current task), `save_memory` (create a new durable memory record with evidence and validity conditions), and `propose_memory_evolution` (the unified successor to the spec's `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` pair, collapsed per spec §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\" into a single tool with an `action` parameter accepting `propose | apply | reject`). The collapse is binding: spec §MCP Surface explicitly says \"audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows.\" Without the unified `propose_memory_evolution`, the new memory surface ships ten tools instead of the audited eight, increasing client cognitive load for no gain.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: `get_task_memory`, `save_memory`, `propose_memory_evolution`, `apply_memory_evolution`, `verify_memory`, `explain_memory`, `list_memory_conflicts`, `consolidate_session`, `get_memory_metrics`, `get_event_trace`.\"\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Each memory record requires: stable id, content, memory class, assertion type, scope, verification status, confidence and confidence reason, freshness policy, validity conditions, invalidation triggers, provenance events, evidence references, linked files, linked symbols, linked docs, linked tests, linked memories, contradiction links, supersession links, access history, usefulness scores, last verified state.\" (spec §Memory Graph)\n\n> \"Adopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\" (spec §A-Mem)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — every memory returned by `get_task_memory` must carry its current verification status; `save_memory` must default to `unverified` and trigger a verification job per spec §Phase 7.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory store, links, evidence, accesses, scores.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/working_memory/` — Phase 5 working-memory operations.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer (emits `MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryInvalidated`).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - Phase 4 retrieval pipeline (used by `get_task_memory` for typed-stream candidate retrieval).\n  - T02 MCP compatibility policy (`docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references; `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface and §Memory Graph in full. Quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `memory_v2/mod.rs` so the rationale for collapsing `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` is preserved at the call site.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/mod.rs` exporting the three tools and the shared request/response types (`TaskMemoryBundle`, `MemoryRecord`, `EvolutionProposal`, `EvolutionAction`).\n3. Implement `get_task_memory` in `memory_v2/get_task_memory.rs`: take `task_id` (resolved through Phase 1 identity) plus optional `intent_hint` and `budget_tokens`; query the Phase 5 working-memory checkpoint for active context; query Phase 3 typed memory streams (`Observation`, `Decision`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, `AntiPattern`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `FailurePattern`, `Procedure`, `Preference`, `ArchitectureInvariant`, `DocsContract`, `OpenQuestion`, `CounterMemory`) for relevant durable claims; rank via the Phase 4 scorer; return a compact `TaskMemoryBundle` where each entry carries inclusion reason, evidence strength, verification status, freshness status, contradiction/supersession state, scope, and an expansion handle per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles \"Every memory-bearing response should identify…\".\n4. Implement `save_memory` in `memory_v2/save_memory.rs`: accept all spec-required memory fields (`content`, `memory_class`, `assertion_type`, `scope`, `confidence`, `confidence_reason`, `freshness_policy`, `validity_conditions`, `invalidation_triggers`, evidence event refs, linked files/symbols/docs/tests). Validate scope-aware filters (spec §Risks \"Scope Leakage\"), default `verification_status = unverified`, persist via Phase 3 store, emit a `MemoryCreated` event via Phase 2 writer, enqueue a verification job per Phase 7, and return the created `MemoryId` plus the resulting `MemoryRecord`.\n5. Implement `propose_memory_evolution` in `memory_v2/propose_memory_evolution.rs` as the unified tool with an `action: \"propose\" | \"apply\" | \"reject\"` parameter (collapsed per spec §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\"). On `propose`, persist a proposal record referencing the source memory and the proposed delta (content change, link addition, supersession, invalidation) without altering durable memory; on `apply`, apply the proposal transactionally, emit `MemoryUpdated`/`MemoryConsolidated` events, and preserve prior state in `memory_evidence` per spec §Memory Graph \"Memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance\"; on `reject`, mark the proposal rejected and emit a consolidation outcome event.\n6. Every tool must emit the appropriate Phase 2 events (`MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryConsolidated`, `MemoryInvalidated`) and stay within the hot-path budget for the tools that participate in the hot path.\n7. Register the three new tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Per the T02 compatibility policy, the spec's nominal `apply_memory_evolution` name continues to respond as a deprecation shim that internally calls `propose_memory_evolution` with `action: \"apply\"`; the shim emits a deprecation warning in the response.\n8. Scope-leak controls: every store query must include `scope` filters at the store layer (spec §Risks \"Scope Leakage\" requires \"scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests\"). Reject any cross-scope leak with a typed error rather than silently returning empty.\n9. In `memory_v2/memory_tools_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert `get_task_memory` surfaces inclusion reason + verification status for every returned memory; (c) assert `save_memory` defaults `verification_status = unverified`, persists every spec field, emits a `MemoryCreated` event, and enqueues a verification job; (d) assert `propose_memory_evolution` with `action = propose` does NOT alter durable memory, `apply` updates transactionally with provenance preserved, `reject` records rejection; (e) assert scope-leak negative tests (a session-scoped query cannot return a branch-scoped memory created in a different branch); (f) assert the `apply_memory_evolution` shim continues to respond and forwards correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/get_task_memory.rs` — `get_task_memory` tool returning compact, ranked, fully-annotated task memory bundles.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/save_memory.rs` — `save_memory` tool persisting new memory with all spec-required fields, defaulting to `unverified`, and enqueueing verification.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/propose_memory_evolution.rs` — unified `propose_memory_evolution` tool with `action: propose | apply | reject`, collapsing the spec's two-tool API per §MCP Surface tool-surface discipline.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/memory_tools_tests.rs` — unit + scope-leak negative tests covering schema, default values, event emission, verification enqueue, transactional apply, and the shim path.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/get_task_memory.rs` — confirms the `get_task_memory` file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/save_memory.rs` — confirms the `save_memory` file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/propose_memory_evolution.rs` — confirms the unified `propose_memory_evolution` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::memory_v2::memory_tools_tests` — every memory-tool test passes (schema, defaults, event emission, transactional apply, scope-leak negatives, shim forwarding).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split per-tool when needed).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: every request/response struct derives `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; the `EvolutionAction` enum is `Serialize`/`Deserialize` with snake_case wire names.\n- No silent error handling — every store/event-writer failure returns a typed error; scope-leak rejections are explicit errors, not empty results.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`EvolutionAction::Apply`, not `EvolutionAction::Do`).\n- For SQLite: all writes go through Phase 3 store APIs; no raw SQL in the RPC layer; transactional apply uses the Phase 3 transaction primitive.\n- Observability: each tool emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `task_id`, `session_id`, `scope`, `memory_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## MCP Surface` (including the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph) and `## Memory Graph` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the unified `propose_memory_evolution` is shipped in this task; the spec's two-tool API is collapsed before MCP contract stabilization per §MCP Surface \"Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\"\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the `apply_memory_evolution` deprecation shim continues to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4253,"cache_read_input_tokens":64195,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"94b8eb29-8d6d-4ba6-bb27-e58f56b043db","request_id":"req_011Cb7MvNok52tFCLxfFdHF5","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2725:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:708:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013qnjbATrjoYikrduhdDr7v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01STKUpHFMmJFdrZGHzbDtUc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T31.md","content":"# T31 — Phase 4 — Compact response shaper + inclusion reasons + expansion handles\n\n**Phase:** 4\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T29, T30\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Phase 5: Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers pipeline steps 9 and 10: deduplicate and compress the ranked candidate set, then return a compact bundle in which **every result carries an `inclusion_reason` and an `expansion_handle`**. Per spec §Phase 4 scope constraint, the shaper's output schema must be forward-compatible with the Phase 5 working-memory checkpoints — Phase 5 layers state semantics on top of these bundles without rewriting the Phase 4 schema. The inclusion-reason discipline is the non-negotiable property the R33 review will check: no bundle ships a result without one, and no result ships an expansion handle that points to a non-existent identity.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Pipeline: ... 9. Deduplicate and compress. 10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason. ... Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\" (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties)\n\n> \"Scope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: ... compact response shaper ...\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T29 `Candidate` stream — supplies the raw set to dedupe and compress.\n  - T30 `RankedCandidate` stream + `DiagnosticMode` — provides scores, inclusion-reason raw material, and the diagnostic toggle.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` and existing context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) — the existing handle generation convention the shaper must match so handles resolve through the same expansion path as `expand_context`.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md` — canonical handle format.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, single source of truth — reuse the existing handle generator, do not invent a parallel one); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (\"compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles\").\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Retrieval Engine pipeline steps 9–10, §Non-Negotiable Product Properties bullets on inclusion reasons and expansion handles, and §Phase 4 scope constraint on forward compatibility with Phase 5 working memory.\n2. Define the bundle schema in `shaper.rs`: `RetrievalBundle { task_id, intent_summary, anchors_summary, results: Vec<BundleResult>, diagnostics: Option<RankingDiagnostics>, budget_report }`. `BundleResult { identity, kind, headline, snippet, inclusion_reason, expansion_handle, source, score }`. `inclusion_reason` and `expansion_handle` are non-optional on `BundleResult`; the type system enforces the spec invariant.\n3. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/inclusion_reasons.rs`: a single small module that converts a `RankedCandidate` into a compact `inclusion_reason` string (≤120 chars) summarizing the top-N contributing signals. In diagnostic mode the full per-signal list is carried separately via `RetrievalBundle.diagnostics`. The compact summary is deterministic given the same inputs (no random tie-break).\n4. Implement deduplication: when two candidates resolve to the same identity, merge their `signal_scores` (take max per signal), union their source set, and keep the highest-scoring `inclusion_reason`. Document the merge policy inline with `## 7. Retrieval Engine` citation.\n5. Implement compression: enforce a global token-budget cap on the bundle by trimming `snippet` length first, then dropping the lowest-scoring trailing results, then setting `BudgetReport::truncated = true`. Never drop a pinned-identity result (the pin set is supplied via a `ShaperContext.pins: HashSet<Identity>` to keep Phase 5 forward compatibility — T35 will populate this).\n6. Generate every `expansion_handle` through the existing `expand_context` handle generator (`rpc/context_cache.rs` / Phase 1 follow-up-handle helper). Assert in debug builds that every handle round-trips through the resolver back to the same identity; assertion failure is a panic, because a bad handle in a shipped bundle is a contract violation.\n7. Forward-compatibility for Phase 5: place `BundleResult` and `RetrievalBundle` in a `pub mod schema` re-exported from `retrieval_v1::mod` so T34's `working_memory::state` can include the bundle verbatim under `selected_memories` and record dropped items under `excluded_memories` with the same `BundleResult` shape. Document this contract with a doc comment citing `## 5. Working Memory` and `## Phase 5: Working Memory`.\n8. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/shaper_tests.rs` covering: every `BundleResult` has a non-empty `inclusion_reason` (string length ≥1, ≤120 in compact mode); every `expansion_handle` resolves back to the original identity; deduplication merges signal scores correctly; compression respects budget without dropping pinned results; diagnostic mode populates `diagnostics`, compact mode does not; serde round-trip preserves the schema unchanged so a Phase 5 checkpoint can include a bundle byte-for-byte; truncation flag is set when results are dropped.\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/shaper.rs` — bundle types, deduplication, compression, expansion-handle wiring.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/inclusion_reasons.rs` — compact `inclusion_reason` generator.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/shaper_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for inclusion-reason presence, handle round-trip, dedupe, compression, pin protection, diagnostic vs compact, serde forward compatibility.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/shaper.rs` — confirms the shaper module exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::shaper_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts inclusion-reason discipline, expansion-handle integrity, and Phase 5 forward compatibility.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Dedup, compression, and handle generation are each their own function.\n- Schema parity: serde derive on `RetrievalBundle` and `BundleResult` matches the in-code field set exactly; any field omitted from JSON (e.g. internal debug counters) is annotated with `#[serde(skip)]` and documented.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: expansion handles use the existing follow-up-handle helper; no parallel handle generator.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; the `inclusion_reason` and `expansion_handle` fields are required on `BundleResult` (no `Option<...>` workaround).\n- Doc citation: the shaper module head cites `## 7. Retrieval Engine` and `## Phase 4: Retrieval V1` for inclusion-reason and expansion-handle requirements; the forward-compatibility comment cites `## 5. Working Memory` and `## Phase 5: Working Memory` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: dedupe, compression, inclusion reasons, expansion handles, diagnostic vs compact mode, and Phase 5 forward compatibility are all delivered in this task — none pushed to Phase 5.\n- [ ] `inclusion_reason` and `expansion_handle` are non-optional fields on `BundleResult`; type system enforces the spec invariant.\n- [ ] Bundle round-trips through serde unchanged so T34's `working_memory_checkpoints` can store it verbatim.\n- [ ] Pin protection is exercised by a test that uses a `ShaperContext.pins` set — even though T35 produces the pins, the interface is in place now.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3868,"cache_read_input_tokens":56046,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3868,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":54,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"98f3bd7b-7d77-4c0e-9ea6-86a24768593a","request_id":"req_011Cb7Myy8pC7jiqXfPtoLpn","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2726:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:711:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016MBNff4Vmm8f1myrXiWNYC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WwRjPQsgfzTC4J4X1QNmPX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T59.md","content":"# T59 — Phase 8 — New memory tools: verify_memory + explain_memory (unified) + list_memory_conflicts\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the unified `verify_explain_memory` MCP tool (collapsing the spec's `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` per §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\") and the new `list_memory_conflicts` tool. The unified tool runs verification checks against a memory and returns both the resulting status (`verified` | `unverified` | `in_review` | `stale` | `contradicted` | `superseded` | `expired` | `invalidated`) and a structured explanation of why each linked file/symbol/doc/test contributed to that status. `list_memory_conflicts` surfaces all contradiction and supersession edges anchored on a target memory or graph node so an operator can review them. Both tools are required by spec §MCP Surface and are the trust surface for the verification engine — without them, the Phase 7 verifier produces statuses that no assistant or operator can interrogate.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: …`verify_memory`, `explain_memory`, `list_memory_conflicts`…\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: …audit whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Verification outputs: `verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`.\" (spec §Verification Engine)\n\n> \"Every memory-bearing response should identify: why the memory was included; evidence strength; verification status; freshness status; contradiction/supersession state; scope; expansion target.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — `verify_explain_memory` is the MCP surface for the Phase 7 verifier; its outputs must match the Phase 7 status taxonomy exactly.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/` — Phase 7 verifier (existence, span, scope, expiry checks; incremental verification jobs).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory store + links + evidence + accesses + scores.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer (emits `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryInvalidated`).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - Phase 1 identity types.\n  - T58 shared `MemoryRecord` type.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references; `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface and §Verification Engine in full. Quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` so the rationale for unifying `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` is preserved at the call site.\n2. Implement `verify_explain_memory` in `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` as the unified tool. Accept `memory_id: MemoryId` plus optional `mode: \"verify\" | \"explain\" | \"verify_and_explain\"` (default `verify_and_explain`). Internally run the Phase 7 verifier on the memory, then construct an `ExplainReport` that itemizes each verification check (linked file exists, linked symbol exists, cited doc exists, linked test exists, evidence span still matches, contradiction state coherent, scope leak absent, time-bound expiry honored) and the outcome per check. Return a `VerifyExplainResponse` with `status: VerificationStatus`, `checks: Vec<CheckResult>` (each carrying `kind`, `target`, `outcome`, `evidence_ref`), `confidence_delta`, and an `expansion_handle` so the caller can pull richer context.\n3. Mode semantics: `verify` returns only the status + a minimal summary; `explain` skips re-running checks and returns the last persisted `ExplainReport`; `verify_and_explain` runs verification and returns the freshly-derived `ExplainReport`. Default is `verify_and_explain` because spec §Verification Engine and §MCP Tool Contract Principles together require fresh, explainable status on every memory surface.\n4. Implement `list_memory_conflicts` in `memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs`. Accept `anchor: MemoryId | FileId | SymbolId | DocSectionId`, traverse `contradicts` and `supersedes` link types from Phase 3 memory_links, and return a paginated list of `ConflictRecord`s each carrying `source`, `target`, `link_type` (`contradicts` | `supersedes`), `link_strength`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `link_verification_status`, and `reason`. Honor scope filters per spec §Risks \"Scope Leakage.\"\n5. Both tools must emit appropriate Phase 2 events: `verify_explain_memory` emits `MemoryRetrieved` plus `MemoryInvalidated` when status transitions away from `verified`; `list_memory_conflicts` emits `MemoryRetrieved` for each surfaced conflict.\n6. Both tools must support compact + full + diagnostic render modes per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles. Compact mode for `verify_explain_memory` returns status + one-line per failed check; diagnostic mode returns the full check trace.\n7. Register the two tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Per the T02 compatibility policy, the spec's nominal `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` names continue to respond as deprecation shims that forward to `verify_explain_memory` with the appropriate `mode`. The shims emit a deprecation warning in the response.\n8. In `memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert `verify_explain_memory` returns the exact Phase 7 status taxonomy and never invents a new status string; (c) assert every check result carries an evidence reference; (d) assert a memory whose linked symbol was deleted transitions to `stale` with a `linked_symbol_missing` check failure; (e) assert `list_memory_conflicts` returns both `contradicts` and `supersedes` edges with correct directionality; (f) assert scope-leak negative tests; (g) assert the `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond and forward correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — unified verify+explain tool with `mode` parameter, full check trace, and expansion handle, per spec §MCP Surface tool-surface discipline.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — `list_memory_conflicts` traversing contradiction + supersession links with scope-aware pagination.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs` — unit + scope-leak negative tests covering schema, status taxonomy parity with Phase 7, evidence-reference completeness, conflict directionality, and shim forwarding.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — confirms the unified verify+explain file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — confirms the `list_memory_conflicts` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::memory_v2::verify_explain_tests` — every verify/explain/conflicts test passes (schema, status parity, evidence completeness, conflict directionality, scope-leak negatives, shim forwarding).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `VerifyExplainResponse`, `CheckResult`, `ConflictRecord` derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; the `mode` enum is serde-tagged with snake_case wire names; the `VerificationStatus` enum reuses the Phase 7 type rather than declaring a parallel one.\n- No silent error handling — every verifier failure returns a typed error; scope-leak rejections are explicit errors, not empty results.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`CheckResult::evidence_ref`, not `CheckResult::ref_str`).\n- For SQLite: all queries go through Phase 3 store and Phase 7 verifier APIs; no raw SQL in the RPC layer.\n- Observability: each tool emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `memory_id`, `scope`, `status`, `check_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## MCP Surface`, `## Verification Engine`, and `## MCP Tool Contract Principles` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the unified `verify_explain_memory` ships in this task; the spec's two-tool API is collapsed before MCP contract stabilization per §MCP Surface.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4764,"cache_read_input_tokens":68448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4764,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6925f9b8-658b-4628-9f96-5512a5babaa4","request_id":"req_011Cb7MzXVz1xvApfw62RZzy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
